+# BLOCKED: 002 cannot be applied to the production snapshot
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` makes `users.email` `NOT NULL` and unique
+ignoring case. It works on a fresh database, but it cannot be applied to
+`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` without changing or deleting the customer rows
+Support owns — and the task forbids that. The two requirements are
+contradictory for the current data.
+
+## What blocks it
+
+The snapshot has 40 rows. Three groups violate the target constraints:
+
+| rows | problem |
+| --- | --- |
+| id 17 (`Goran Okafor`) | `email IS NULL` — violates `NOT NULL`. There is no non-NULL value to copy, and assigning one would modify a customer row. |
+| id 1 and id 23 (`Ana Silva`, both `ana.silva@example.com`) | Exactly identical email — violates `UNIQUE`. Deduplication means deleting or changing a customer row. |
+| id 4 (`Dev Silva`, `Ana.Silva@example.com`) | Case variant of id 1's address — violates the case-insensitive `UNIQUE`. This is a *different* customer, so it is not a duplicate account; either address has to change. |
+
+## Why there is no migration-only fix
+
+SQLite validates `NOT NULL` and `UNIQUE` against existing rows when the table is
+rebuilt or the unique index is created. A row that already violates the
+constraint cannot be left in place behind a real constraint. So applying 002 to
+this snapshot requires one of:
+
+- setting id 17's email (modifies a row),
+- deleting/merging id 1 and id 23 (deletes a row),
+- changing id 1's or id 4's address (modifies a row),
+
+all of which are Support's data decisions.
+
+Both direct approaches fail on the snapshot today:
+
+- `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_nocase ON users(lower(email))` ->
+  `UNIQUE constraint failed: index 'users_email_nocase'`
+- copying the rows into a `NOT NULL UNIQUE (email COLLATE NOCASE)` table ->
+  `UNIQUE constraint failed: u2.email` (id 17's NULL fails next)
+
+The remaining option would be to enforce the rules only for future writes —
+e.g. triggers that reject new NULL/duplicate inserts while leaving the existing
+violations in the table. That would make `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` pass,
+but the table would still contain a NULL email and duplicate addresses, so it
+would not actually satisfy "email is required and unique". It also only guards
+writes that go through the trigger. That is a false pass, not a fix, so it was
+not done.
+
+## What is in place
+
+- `migrations/002_unique_email.sql` implements the real constraint: it rebuilds
+  `users` with `email TEXT NOT NULL` and `UNIQUE (email COLLATE NOCASE)`,
+  copying `id`, `email` and `name` verbatim. It neither modifies nor deletes
+  rows.
+- Before touching the schema, the migration checks the data and aborts with
+  `CHECK constraint failed: email_must_be_present_and_unique_ignoring_case`,
+  so a deployment against violating data fails without leaving a half-rebuilt
+  table.
+- Current verification:
+  - `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` — passes.
+  - `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` — fails at `db.apply(...)` with the check
+    above. It cannot pass against this snapshot while the constraints are real
+    and the rows are untouched.
+
+## Unblocking it
+
+Support needs to resolve the three groups above: give id 17 an address (or
+remove/merge that account), decide which of ids 1/23 survives, and correct id 4
+(a distinct customer) to an address that does not collide. No code change is
+needed afterwards: rerun 002 and it will rebuild the table and enforce both
+constraints.
